{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q015_artifact_tag_not_homology","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, d1a14h2 (sunid 343393, PDB 1a14 chain H residues 1-8) is not a globular protein domain. It is an N-terminal tag segment classified in class l (Artifacts) > fold l.1 (Tags, sunid 310573) > superfamily l.1.1 (Tags, sunid 310607) > family l.1.1.1 (Tags, sunid 310682) > protein 'N-terminal Tags' (sunid 310894) > species 'Synthetic' (sunid 311501). The other segment, d1a14h1 (H:9-111, sunid 20061), is a real domain in b.1.1.1 (an immunoglobulin-type variable domain, in the same entry as the L-chain domain d1a14l_, also b.1.1.1). The 1a14 entry also has d1a14n_ in b.68.1.1.

Similarity to d1a14h2 should NOT be treated as protein homology evidence. The 2.08 homology response for d1a14h2 has homology_implied_by_level=false and the warning 'artifact_not_homology_evidence': 'Class l contains artificial sequence artifacts; similarity to these records does not imply biological homology.' Here the 'fold', 'superfamily' and 'family' labels under class l are bookkeeping groupings of artificial sequences. They do not assert common ancestry, and shared membership means only that the segments are tag-like. Matching a short, low-complexity tag-like segment (8 residues) says nothing about shared evolutionary origin, function or structure of the host proteins. This applies in both directions: a hit to the tag tells you nothing about the real domain, and a tag at the start of an otherwise unrelated protein should not be used for template selection, function transfer or benchmark homology positives.

